      "text": "In the right angle triangle $SRQ$ in the diagram, we have $SR=9$ and $RQ=\\sqrt{65}$. Let $QS=h$, where $h$ is a point on $QS$ where the respective length of $QS$ relative to $SR$ is the same as the length of $SR$ relative to $SQ$. Since $SR=9$ relative to $S$, and the ratio of $RQ$ to $SR$ is the same as that of $SR$ to $QS$, since $SR$ is the same in both cases, we have\n\n\\[\\dfrac{SR}{QS} = \\dfrac{SR}{SR+h}.\\]\n\nTherefore, we have that $\\dfrac{9}{QS}= \\dfrac{9}{9+h}$. Simplifying the fraction and massaging the equation, we get \n\n$8QS-8h=9h-9^2.$\n\nBy solving the resulting quadratic, we can find $QS=10.5.$ The corresponding quote is $\\boxed{10.5}$.\n\nQuick note: All of this is just a guess, and probably not right!",
